Snowflake Connector vs Iceberg — Key Differences

  ---
  What Each One Is

  Snowflake Connector          Iceberg
  ─────────────────────────    ──────────────────────────────
  Proprietary Snowflake        Open standard format
    protocol                     (Apache project)
  Data goes TO Snowflake       Data stays in YOUR storage
    internal storage             (ADLS, S3, GCS)
  Snowflake owns everything    You own the data files

  ---
  Side by Side Comparison

  ┌────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                        │    Snowflake Connector     │                  Iceberg                   │
  ├────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Data location          │ Snowflake internal storage │ Your ADLS Gen2                             │
  ├────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Format                 │ Snowflake proprietary      │ Open Parquet files                         │
  ├────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Vendor lock-in         │ High — tied to Snowflake   │ Low — any engine can read                  │
  ├────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Who reads it           │ Only Snowflake             │ Databricks, Spark, Flink, Trino, Snowflake │
  ├────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Setup complexity       │ Simple                     │ More steps                                 │
  ├────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Performance            │ Optimized for Snowflake    │ Depends on file layout                     │
  ├────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Cost                   │ Pay Snowflake storage      │ Pay Azure storage (cheaper)                │
  ├────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Time travel            │ Snowflake manages          │ Iceberg manages natively                   │
  ├────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Schema evolution       │ Snowflake handles          │ Iceberg handles                            │
  ├────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Writes from Databricks │ Via JDBC/Spark connector   │ Via Iceberg catalog                        │
  └────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  ---
  Data Flow Comparison

  Snowflake Connector:
  Databricks
    └─► Snowflake Connector (JDBC)
          └─► Snowflake Internal Storage  ← data locked here
                └─► Only Snowflake can query

  Iceberg:
  Databricks
    └─► Writes Parquet to ADLS  ← YOU own this data
          └─► Iceberg metadata (catalog)
                ├─► Snowflake can query ✅
                ├─► Databricks can query ✅
                ├─► Spark can query ✅
                └─► Any Iceberg engine ✅

  ---
  When to Use Which

  Use Snowflake Connector when:
  ✅ Snowflake is your only query engine
  ✅ You want simplest possible setup
  ✅ No need to share data with other engines
  ✅ Quick POC / prototype
  ✅ Already fully committed to Snowflake

  Use Iceberg when:
  ✅ Multiple engines need same data (Databricks + Snowflake)
  ✅ You want to avoid vendor lock-in
  ✅ Data must stay in your own storage
  ✅ Compliance requires data ownership
  ✅ Cost matters — Azure storage cheaper than Snowflake storage
  ✅ Production architecture for the long term

  ---
  For Your POC Specifically

  Your requirement was:
    "Databricks writes → Snowflake reads"
    "Iceberg tables in Snowflake"

  Snowflake Connector:
    ✅ Works but data goes into Snowflake storage
    ❌ Not truly Iceberg — Snowflake just stores it internally
    ❌ Databricks cannot read the same files back as Iceberg

  True Iceberg approach:
    ✅ Data files in ADLS (you own them)
    ✅ Both Databricks AND Snowflake read same Parquet files
    ✅ Switch from Snowflake to another engine anytime
    ✅ This is the correct architecture for your requirement

  ---
  Bottom Line

  Snowflake Connector = Easy but locked in
  Iceberg             = More setup but open, portable, future-proof

  For your stated goal — Databricks writes, Snowflake reads, data in ADLS — Iceberg is the right approach. The Snowflake Connector was a workaround for the Maven JAR issue we hit, not the ideal solution.

